SP  - 43
EP  - 48
DO  - 10.1109/ICSET65917.2025.11283708
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032136752?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid growth of augmented reality (AR) in fashion has created opportunities for more immersive and sustainable online shopping experiences. However, challenges such as poor size visualization, high return rates, and limited garment realism remain. This paper presents a mobile-based virtual fitting room that integrates 3D avatars, GAN-based fabric texture enhancement, AR visualization, and social sharing. Developed through an incremental prototype approach, the system progressed across four versions, from 3D avatar creation to AR integration and social network sharing. Usability testing achieved a System Usability Scale (SUS) score of 82.67, while additional evaluations confirmed efficient model loading times, garment fit accuracy within 1.8 cm, and significant improvements in texture realism using Real-ESRGAN. The study also highlights technical challenges-including computational requirements, scalability, and dataset limitations-as well as ethical considerations related to body measurement data and privacy. By addressing these gaps, the prototype demonstrates how AR and GAN technologies can be combined in a unified, mobile-based platform to enhance user confidence, reduce waste, and support responsible fashion consumption. This work advances digital fashion technology while contributing to SDG 9 (Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure) and SDG 12 (Responsible Consumption and Production). © 2025 IEEE.
