AB  - Interactive 3D visualization technologies and virtual reality (VR) have improved learning environments and experiences, especially for complex subjects like anatomy instruction. When it comes to encouraging interactive student involvement, VR-based solutions outperform conventional training methods. Research on Embodied Virtual Assistants (EVAs) in the context of anatomy instruction that make use of generative AI and spoken communication is, nevertheless, underrepresented. To facilitate verbal communication and support participants in answering questions on varied cognitive complexity anatomy, this study presents a VR environment with a generative AI-embodied virtual assistant in this work. We conducted a preliminary user study with 16 individuals to evaluate the suggested environment's usability and technical efficacy. For the avatar- and screen-based virtual assistant arrangement, we carried out a two-level within-subject design. The study would be beneficial to medical and health science students, anatomy instructors and educational technologists. Furthermore, developers of VR-based educational tools and researchers in human-computer interaction and AI-driven learning systems would also benefit from their benefits. Future works for this study could involve scaling the system for larger participant groups, making it suitable to be implemented in several universities or colleges for anatomy education. © 2025 IEEE.
