AB  - Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) is emerging as a transformative tool in education, offering the potential to reshape traditional learning experiences. In parallel, recent advancements in mixed reality (MR) have the potential to create a unique learning environment that seamlessly blends physical and artificial entities, fostering immersive and interactive educational experiences. Despite the promise of deploying embodied GenAI in MR settings for enhancing learning experiences, empirical research examining its roles in learning processes remains limited. This study examines the process of knowledge construction through learners' interactions with an embodied GenAI agent in MR learning environments. The study examines 1162 interactions collected from twenty-six graduate students participating in an interactive learning session on AI ethics in the MR learning environment with an embodied GenAI agent. Using a learning analytics approach combined with process mining techniques, we mapped the sequential patterns of knowledge construction processes observed in this smart learning environment. Findings indicate that real-time feedback and adaptive interactions from embodied GenAI agents enhance engagement and facilitate an iterative cycle of inquiry for knowledge construction. Our study has practical implications for the design and implementation of educational technologies, guiding the effective incorporation of GenAI-driven interactive agents into MR for next-generation smart learning environments. © 2025 IEEE.
