DO  - 10.1109/ICECET63943.2025.11472331
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037209960?origin=resultslist
AB  - In the context of recruitment, job interview methods frequently face difficulties in objectively evaluating candidates' soft skills and personality traits. For instance, traditional interviews often rely on human observations, which can be biased and inconsistent with each individual. The purpose of this study is to introduce an innovative Virtual Reality based recruitment system that addresses these challenges. The methodology involves integrating biometric gaze analysis, voice analysis, gesture, and body language analysis along side the virtual reality scenarios to facilitate an assessment of job candidates. Biometric gaze analysis allows recruiters to measure the level of focus through eyes as well as stress level through heart rate and temperature. Voice analysis component analyzes different voice patterns such as stuttering, repeats, and blocks which gives insights on candidate's confidence and emotional states. Gesture and body language analysis captures four different non-verbal communication cues which are steepled hands, open palm, self referencing gestures, and an upright open posture. These poses will be utilized when understanding how candidates display confidence in public speaking, using a CNN-based model (Xception) for gesture and body language analysis.The findings of this study indicate that this approach significantly enhances recruitment accuracy by evaluating candidates through their natural interactions and behaviors. By using this proposed system, companies can improve their hiring processes particularly in industries where soft skills are paramount. As the job market continues to evolve, leveraging innovative solutions can provide a competitive advantage in recruiting and retaining top talent. © 2025 IEEE.
