AB  - This paper reports the preliminary work of an alternative approach of establishing virtual content for AR-assisted assembly. The assembly of aviation products demands extreme precision, while traditional guidance using text and 2D drawings is prone to human errors. Augmented Reality (AR) offers a visual solution but is hindered by the labor-intensive process of creating AR content. Large Language Models (LLMs) can be used to automate this process. The approach involves parsing assembly instructions to automatically extract key information on components, tools, and procedures. This structured data is then associated with three-dimensional (3D) models and used to generate scene description documents in a predefined JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) format. These documents drive a unified AR system that visually guides the operators. The evaluation demonstrates that the novel approach proposed in this paper cuts compilation time by more than 39% against the traditional pipeline. This illuminates the prospect that the LLMs-powered method substantially accelerates content generation, enhancing the viability and efficiency of AR-assisted assembly in complex aviation manufacturing. © 2025 IEEE.
