AB  - Construction waste has long accounted a substantial proportion of industrial waste, with demolition wood waste often subjected to industrialized recycling processes that significantly degraded the inherent qualities of timber, including its grain, texture, and material character. In contrast to conventional approaches that prioritize material standardization, this study emphasizes the preservation and reinterpretation of the intrinsic properties of reclaimed timber as a design driver within interior design contexts. This research proposes a digital upcycling workflow that integrates AI-assisted generative design with mixed reality–based interaction, enabling a more intuitive and simplified approach to the circular reuse of demolition wood waste for interior design applications. Through real-time visualization, material inspection, and iterative design adjustment, designers are able to directly engage with material irregularities and spatial constraints, aligning design intent with the physical reality of reclaimed timber. The proposed framework includes waste timber and interior site collection, material and site database construction, computational and AR-assisted generative design, parameter and unit definition, and final interior spatial generation and design validation. By situating demolition wood waste within a digitally mediated interior design workflow, this study reframes reclaimed timber not as a standardized construction material, but as a material with distinctive spatial, tactile, and experiential value, supporting a shift from industrial recycling toward design-led digital upcycling practices in interior environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
