VL  - 76
DO  - 10.1016/j.aei.2026.104815
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043537041?origin=resultslist
AB  - Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) have demonstrated exceptional capabilities in environmental sensing and autonomous navigation across diverse real-world scenarios. However, in highly dynamic and human-interactive environments, UAVs still require effective human–robot collaboration to achieve shared autonomy (SA), balancing safety, efficiency, and operational awareness. Current SA approaches fail to adequately integrate human control intent while maintaining computational efficiency and user-friendly interfaces. This paper presents an Advanced Collaborative System (ACS) that addresses these challenges through two complementary innovations. First, we propose a dual-stage SA policy that integrates human control intent into both path planning and trajectory optimization. A human intent based potential field (HIPF) guides front-end path search, while a human–robot control intent consistency evaluation function (HICEF) ensures trajectory-intent alignment during back-end optimization. Second, we develop a portable Mixed Reality (MR) interface integrated with Large Language Model (LLM)-powered speech recognition, enabling intuitive UAV status visualization and natural language command interpretation. Comprehensive simulations and real-world experiments demonstrate that the proposed ACS achieves 96.63% lateral intent consistency success rate while maintaining real-time performance (7.34 ms average planning time). The system successfully enables context-aware UAV navigation in cluttered environments, ensuring trajectory compliance with human intent. Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.
