VL  - 305
DO  - 10.1016/j.eswa.2025.130881
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105026664232?origin=resultslist
AB  - Conventional music education suffers from a lack of participation and student engagement, and leads to poor learning outcomes. Therefore, the present research provides an immersive e-learning model that combines deep learning (DL) techniques and virtual reality (VR) technology to improve music education. The system includes a VR immersive environment equipped with multi-modal sensors such as motion sensors, optical tracking, and hand-tracking sensors for performance monitoring. The Timbre-Aware Synchrosqueezing Time-Frequency Transform (TASTFT), Transformer with Separable Convolutions (TranSepNet), and Spatio-temporal Graph Neural Network (STGNN) are used to retrieve audio, visual, and motion features, respectively. Adaptive, real-time, and pedagogically relevant feedback for rhythm, pitch, and gesture accuracy is generated by the Reinforcement Learning-Conditional Generative Adversarial Network (RL-CGAN). The system was tested with 543 undergraduate students from three cities, i.e., Beijing, Nanjing, and Shanghai in China. The behavioural study revealed that beginner learners have higher engagement and interaction than advanced users, and there were instrument-specific variations for the drum, violin, guitar, and piano practice. The multimodal DL system achieved better results with 98.78% accuracy, 97.47% precision, 96.27% recall, and 96.86% F1-score, and 96.78% specificity and was superior to the existing methods. In sum, the system provides a promising solution to boost user engagement and learning efficiency in music education. © 2025 Published by Elsevier Ltd.
