AB  - Generative artificial intelligence has enabled more natural and context-aware human–computer interactions. While most conversational AI systems are accessed through traditional desktop interfaces, immersive virtual reality environments introduce different interaction conditions that may influence user experience. However, it remains unclear how the same generative conversational agent is perceived across these contexts. This study compares user experience when interacting with an identical generative AI conversational system in a virtual reality environment and through a standard computer interface. The analysis focuses on dialogue, naturalness, satisfaction, trust, cognitive effort, and sense of presence. Participants perform equivalent conversational tasks in both conditions, ensuring that differences arise solely from the interaction medium. Preliminary observations suggest that virtual reality enhances presence and emotional engagement, while desktop interaction offers greater familiarity and control. These findings highlight the role of immersion in shaping user experience with generative AI systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
