AB  - Reclaimed timber from demolition waste often loses its intrinsic material value due to standardized industrial recycling. This research proposes a computational upcycling workflow that addresses the geometric heterogeneity of waste wood by integrating AI-aided generative ideation with Mixed Reality (MR)-assisted fabrication. The framework utilizes photogrammetry for high-fidelity material digitization and 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS)for environment-scale scanning, creating a parametric database framework designed to encode morphological and textural features as design drivers. A diffusion-based generative model is conceptualized to translate these material identities into spatial concepts, which are subsequently mapped onto physical constraints within a Grasshopper-mediated environment. By establishing a bi-directional feedback loop between generative visual motifs and MR-based site interaction, the workflow enables designers to navigate the challenges of irregular geometries with minimal latency. This study proposes and preliminarily evaluates a computational framework, representing a shift from non-standard materials to a data-driven approach, offering an intuitive yet technically robust methodology for the circular reuse oftimber in interior upcycling. © 2026, Education and research in Computer Aided Architectural Design in Europ. All rights reserved.
