AB  - Effective VR safety training needs context-aware regulation during task execution, not just immersive visuals or instructional feedback. We present CARED-VR, a context-aware VR training prototype that integrates a vision-language model (VLM) with haptic gloves to deliver graded reinforcement and enforceable safety constraints in a simulated power-drilling task. The VLM functions as a runtime supervisory layer that evaluates visually observable configurations, including drill-bit and material compatibility and ongoing tool use, against predefined safety conditions. Safe actions proceed uninterrupted, while unsafe configurations trigger a structured escalation of interventions: verbal guidance, simulated physical consequences, and haptic enforcement that physically prevents unsafe operation. We developed CARED-VR, a simulated workshop in which users perform drilling tasks on different materials within a virtual environment (VE) using the SenseGlove Nova 1 in conjunction with a Valve Index headset to enable immersive, haptically augmented interaction. The system illustrates how high-level contextual reasoning can be coupled with low-level embodied interaction to move beyond passive simulation toward execution-time safety control. In addition, CARED-VR serves as a research platform for examining user compliance, workload, and perceived realism. © 2026 IEEE.
