TI  - Enhancing Interactive Experiences in Virtual Cultural Heritage through Gen AI: A PLS-SEM Empirical Investigation Focusing on Generation Z
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence, Virtual Reality and Interaction Design, AIVRID 2026
SP  - 669
EP  - 674
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3820915.3821017
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050607691?origin=resultslist
AB  - Against the backdrop of accelerated digitalization of cultural heritage and the rapid advancement of smart tourism, how generative artificial intelligence and virtual reality technologies can enhance Generation Z tourists' engagement experiences and value co-creation warrants in-depth investigation. Grounded in the Stimulus-Organism-Response(S-O-R) theoretical framework, this study develops a research model of "creative design, algorithmic trust, and social presence → emotional resonance → value co-creation,"focusing on generative AI-empowered virtual reality cultural heritage (VRCH)contexts. Targeting Generation Z tourists, an offline VRCH experiential experiment was conducted, perceptual data were collected through structured questionnaires, and the proposed model was tested using partial least squares structural equation modeling(PLS-SEM). The results indicate that(1)creative design, algorithmic trust, and social presence each exert significant positive effects on emotional resonance, with social presence demonstrating the strongest influence and emerging as the most salient stimulus driving Generation Z's emotional responses;(2)emotional resonance significantly and positively affects value co-creation, suggesting that emotional arousal serves as a crucial psychological prerequisite for Generation Z's transition from "experience consumers"to "co-creators";(3)emotional resonance significantly mediates the relationships between the three categories of stimuli and value co-creation, thereby elucidating the internal mechanism through which generative AI interaction features translate into co-creative behaviors via affective processes. Theoretically, this study extends the application of the S-O-R framework and value co-creation research within generative AI-enabled virtual cultural heritage contexts; practically it offers implications for the design of virtual cultural heritage systems and generative AI interactions tailored to Generation Z. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
