AB  - Architectural assembly often involves dynamic, uncertain, and spatially complex conditions, yet traditional robotic systems rely on fixed programming and screen-based interfaces that separate human intuition from robotic execution. This study investigates a unified workflow for human-AI-robot collaborative design in immersive mixed-reality environments, aiming to bridge the gap between generative design intelligence and precise robotic assembly. We propose an integrated system where augmented reality enables designers to manipulate virtual components in real space, conditional Generative Adversarial Networks (cGANs) generate context-aware modular assembly proposals, and robotic arms execute precise physical assembly through real-time bidirectional communication. A paireddataset approach and image-based representation allow the cGAN to learn modular relationships, while a digital twin ensures continuous feedback and low-latency interaction. Experimental results demonstrate that the system supports both manual and AI-assisted workflows, achieving accurate alignment of modular components and enabling iterative co-creation. The approach captures designers’ tacit knowledge and integrates it into the generative model, creating a synergistic loop between human spatial reasoning, AI prediction, and robotic actuation. Findings suggest that immersive mixed-reality interfaces combined with data-driven reasoning can transform architectural robotics from reactive control to adaptive, collaborative, and co-creative design-toproduction processes. © 2026 and published by the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA), Hong Kong.
