AB  - Recent advances in Virtual Reality, conversational AI, and multimodal interaction have opened new opportunities for enhancing visitor engagement in cultural heritage settings. This paper investigates a conversational agent for virtual art exploration that integrates real-time gaze tracking, vision-language models (VLMs), and structured semantic knowledge to support adaptive dialogue in a virtual art exhibition. Gaze coordinates serve as an implicit signal of user attention, guiding VLM-based visual interpretation and enabling context-aware responses grounded in a domain-specific knowledge graph. We report preliminary findings across three evaluation stages: (1) an initial assessment of VLM performance on gaze-driven visual interpretation with and without semantic context; (2) a qualitative comparison of a gaze-informed agent with a manually annotated Areas of Interest (AOI) baseline; and (3) a between-subjects user study with 27 participants examining enjoyment, engagement, personalization, collaboration, and gaze awareness. Results suggest that gaze-driven AOI inference is a viable alternative to manually predefined AOIs: it often produces more fine-grained, gaze-contingent interpretations while maintaining comparable user experience to the baseline condition. These findings motivate further investigation into semantically grounded, gaze-responsive conversational agents as a potential approach to personalized interaction in immersive cultural environments. © 2026 The Authors.
