TI  - HINT: A Bayesian RAG-LAM Framework for Real-Time Semantic Communication in XR Services
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Communications Magazine
VL  - 64
IS  - 5
SP  - 116
EP  - 123
DO  - 10.1109/MCOM.001.2500408
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037512271?origin=resultslist
AB  - Immersive extended reality (XR) is a key 6G use case, but its ultra-high bandwidth and milli-second latency requirements clash with the high inference cost of large AI models (LAMs). These models enable semantic communication by inferring user intent from multimodal signals and transmitting only task-relevant information, yet their computation time creates an intelligence latency paradox. This article introduces HINT (Hierarchical Intent-aware Network for XR Transmission), a tutorial system-oriented framework that explains how to implement a Bayesian retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline for XR. HINT decomposes cognition into a fast edge tier (System 1) and a slower cloud tier (System 2). At the edge, a low-latency cognitive reflex arc uses Bayesian RAG over a compact intent knowledge base to handle frequent, high-confidence interactions. An uncertainty trigger decides when an input is too ambiguous and must be escalated to a cloud-hosted large model for deeper reasoning. We outline design decisions, including the intent vector structure, the organization and update of the edge knowledge base, and the mapping from intent to asymmetric transmission and rendering policies for 3D Gaussian splatting scenes. Simulation results in a Unity-based XR environment indicate that HINT can reduce bandwidth and client-side load while keeping intent latency within interactive bounds. We conclude with open challenges and practical guidelines for deploying Bayesian RAG at the edge in future 6G-native XR systems. © 1979-2012 IEEE.
