DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-31421-5_7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045591227?origin=resultslist
AB  - Recent advancements in generative AI and spatial computing offer transformative potential for integrating AI-assisted generation into early-stage design. However, the usability of text-to-image systems within immersive environments—offering true scale and spatial depth—remains underexplored for large-scale transportation design. This study investigates design behaviors and workflow implications of AI-driven image generation on Apple Vision Pro, focusing on biomimetic vehicle design. We conducted a quasi-experimental study with six participants (two experts and four novices) who performed shark- and eagle-inspired motorcycle design tasks in VR. Leveraging the Vision Pro’s high-resolution spatial display and head-tracking, participants iteratively refined designs using voice prompts and freehand sketching. Data from observations and semi-structured interviews were qualitatively analyzed to identify interaction patterns and designer-AI intent mismatches. Results indicate that while immersive VR significantly enhances scale perception and proportion assessment, current input modalities (voice and sketch) lack the precision of traditional desktop workflows. These findings suggest that future immersive design tools must bridge the gap between spatial visualization advantages and the need for high-precision input hardware. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
