AB  - Background: Fibromyalgia syndrome (FMS) affects approximately 2.1% (95% CI: 1.5–3.0%) of the global population[21], characterized by widespread chronic pain, cognitive dysfunction, and significant quality of life impairment. Traditional Tai Chi has demonstrated efficacy in managing fibromyalgia symptoms, while virtual reality (VR) technologies offer immersive rehabilitation environments. Objective: This study presents the design and feasibility validation of an innovative VR-based Tai Chi rehabilitation system integrating large language model (LLM) assistance and gamification elements, with cognitive load assessment using 32-channel electroencephalography (EEG). Methods: We developed a Unity-based VR application featuring adaptive Tai Chi instruction, real-time LLM-powered guidance, and comprehensive reward mechanisms. A pilot study with 24 participants (12 fibromyalgia patients, 12 healthy controls) evaluated system usability via the System Usability Scale (SUS) and cognitive engagement through 32-channel EEG recording analyzing alpha, beta, theta power spectral density, frontal alpha asymmetry, and cognitive load indices. Results: The system achieved favorable usability scores (mean SUS = 82.5 ± 6.3) exceeding established benchmarks. Subjective workload was moderate (NASA-TLX = 42.3 ± 8.7) and simulator sickness was mild-to-moderate (SSQ total = 12.4 ± 8.6). EEG analysis revealed significant sensorimotor mu rhythm desynchronization (C3: −42.3 ± 8.7%, d = 1.82; C4: −38.9 ± 9.2%, d = 1.65), enhanced frontal theta power indicating focused attention, a post-session posterior alpha increase (+18.7%, d = 0.69) interpreted as a post-exercise relaxation/recovery signature, and a positive frontal alpha asymmetry shift (d = 2.80) suggesting approach motivation and positive emotional engagement. Visual Analog Scale (VAS) pain scores decreased by 16.3 points (95% CI: 11.4–21.2), exceeding the minimal clinically important difference (MCID) threshold; FIQ-R total decreased by 8.5 points (95% CI: −12.8 to − 4.2; MCID met), and 7/12 (58.3%) FM participants achieved ≥ 30% VAS reduction. Cognitive load indices demonstrated appropriate task demands without overwhelming users. Conclusion: This LLM-assisted VR Tai Chi system is feasible and demonstrates favorable usability and appropriate cognitive engagement patterns in a short-duration pilot with 12 FM patients and 12 healthy controls. Sustained analgesic efficacy cannot be inferred from immediate post-session data and requires confirmation in an adequately powered controlled trial. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
