AB  - Immersive Virtual Reality (IVR) has emerged as a transformative tool in education, providing interactive learning experiences. A key challenge in IVR-based educational environments is the provision of real-time instructional guidance, traditionally delivered by human instructors, whose availability limits scalability. This study investigates the integration of a Large Language Model (LLM)-based AI agent to deliver instructional guidance in a STEM learning scenario, where participants engage in programming tasks requiring both physical and cognitive activity. To assess the agent’s performance, we perform an automated evaluation, which indicates high performance, with an average score of 9.76±0.43 out of 10 in relevance, faithfulness, completeness, clarity, and logical flow. In addition, participants from diverse educational backgrounds are included in a within-subjects study to provide their perceptions. Overall, the results indicate that LLM-based guidance is generally perceived as engaging, helpful, and acceptable. When compared with human instructors, no significant difference is found in the need for post-guidance assistance, suggesting that AI support sufficiently enables learners to understand tasks and concepts. Notably, most participants agree that the LLM agent performs well in providing clear and structured explanations and offering quick responses to their questions, demonstrating its strong potential to deliver step-by-step guidance and effectively support the learning process. Finally, participants’ feedback identifies areas for improvement, highlighting opportunities to make AI-driven support more personalized and effective in IVR learning environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
