AB  - The construction industry is undergoing a digital transformation driven by computational design, digital fabrication, and artificial intelligence. Timber, as a renewable and lightweight material, offers strong potential for low-carbon and prefabricated construction; however, conventional timber jointing systems lack the flexibility to accommodate irregular geometries or non-standard material streams. At the same time, emerging AI-assisted design tools and human–machine collaboration (HMC) workflows are enabling more intuitive and precise assembly processes. This paper presents a novel workflow that integrates AI-based 3D reconstruction, generative joint synthesis, and mixed-reality (MR) assembly to enable the structural use of irregular natural timber such as branches. The method digitally captures each branch through photogrammetry, generates customised joint geometries using multi-view AI prompting, and fabricates the connectors via 3D printing. Assembly is supported through MR guidance using HoloLens, enabling precise human–machine collaboration without conventional drawings. Results from a proof-of concept structure demonstrate that the workflow can reliably translate biological irregularity into a buildable system, reducing manual modelling effort and lowering assembly errors. The approach highlights the potential of AI-augmented fabrication to expand material circularity and enable adaptive construction using non-standard natural timber components. © 2026 and published by the Association for Computer-Aided Architectural Design Research in Asia (CAADRIA), Hong Kong.
