AB  - The progressive aging of the global population increases the prevalence of mild cognitive impairment (MCI), challenging healthcare systems to support functional independence in daily activities at home. This work presents a privacy-preserving assistive system that integrates multimodal AI with lightweight augmented reality to deliver personalized, step-by-step guidance for instrumental activities of daily living. The architecture orchestrates speech recognition, Retrieval-Augmented Generation, and computer vision through Large Language Models and Vision–Language Models to provide contextualized assistance. A key contribution is an adaptive closed-loop interaction mechanism that estimates task progress from visual feedback, verifies step completion through VLM-based action recognition, and dynamically adjusts guidance pacing. To increase data protection, raw audio–visual streams are processed locally at the edge for transcription and verification, while only textual metadata are transmitted to the cloud for high-level reasoning and speech synthesis. A feasibility study with older adults aged 59 to 87 years performing tasks in unfamiliar environments demonstrated that the experimental group required significantly fewer assistance requests than controls, 2.50 vs. 4.43 with p < .001 and |g| = 2.14, and reported high satisfaction of 4.48 out of 5. These results provide preliminary evidence of the system’s feasibility as a framework for cognitive assistance and its potential integration into personalized home healthcare workflows. © 2026 The Authors.
