SP  - 208
EP  - 219
PB  - Association for Computing Machinery
DO  - 10.1145/3744916.3764549
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1145/3744916.3764549
AB  - Extended Reality (XR) apps have become increasingly popular across different domains, evidenced by its market growing to over $165 billion in 2024. Despite their widespread usage, XR apps present new challenges for automated software testing approaches, especially for testing script generation. XR apps have complicated action space from multi-device coordination with temporal precision. Besides, natural language instructions in XR apps usually have a large semantic gap with specific XR interactions. Therefore, previous testing script generation approaches on 2D apps such as Android can hardly be adopted into XR apps.To address these challenges, we introduce RealityScript, a domain-specific language designed to formalize spatially distributed, time-sensitive operations across heterogeneous XR devices. The language incorporates synchronized action blocks, device-agnostic input abstractions, and temporal operators to handle the complexities of XR interaction testing to describe spatial and temporal information. Building on RealityScript, we develop RealityCraft, a two-phase framework that translates natural language instructions into executable RealityScript scripts through semantic lifting and device binding. We conduct experiments on 125 XR interaction scenarios across three popular industrial-setting XR apps. Our evaluation shows that RealityCraft significantly outperforms state-of-the-art approaches in XR testing script generation accuracy (achieving nearly twice the BLEU score of the best baseline) and produces scripts with 74.07% replay success in runtime testing, compared to under 20% for existing approaches. This demonstrates the effectiveness of RealityCraft on the testing script generation of XR apps. We make all data public at https://sites.google.com/view/xr-testing-script-synthesis.
