AB  - This contribution presents the results of an applied research project focused on the development and evaluation of an innovative prototype for patient education. The project addresses the growing need for digital educational tools to support chronically ill patients in managing their condition. The prototype targets individuals with chronic kidney disease (CKD), a condition of increasing global prevalence and significance. Evidence suggests that effective patient education can decelerate the progression of kidney function decline in CKD. Central to the project is a prototype that integrates avatars in immersive virtual reality (VR) with an AI-driven language model. The contribution details the system architecture and shares insights from a user-centered, iterative development and evaluation process conducted in close collaboration with healthcare professionals and patients. The prototype enables CKD patients to enter a virtual consultation room, select a digital advisor, and engage in interactive, educational dialogues about SGLT-2 inhibitors, a novel class of drugs relevant to CKD treatment. In a user experience study conducted with ambulatory nephrology patients at a Swiss cantonal hospital, key user-acceptance criteria, as well as visual, interactive, verbal, and non-verbal design parameters, were examined. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
